 "deviations": [
  "Rules 16-21 (the service): the server is a machine: the ball appears at the server's strike point and leaves it with the serve's velocity and spin 3.0 s after the start; there is no opponent, the attempt ends when your return lands",
  "Rule 4 (the racket in the hand): the racket is clamped to the right hand with its handle along the forearm (the hand has no fingers to change the grip); it cannot be dropped or thrown",
  "Rule 12 (a ball touching a line is in): the ball's footprint is one point, the point under its centre at its first contact with the ground (a real ball's footprint is about 5 cm across)",
  "Rule 24 f (deliberately touching the ball twice, carrying it): intention cannot be judged: racket contacts less than 0.05 s apart are one hit, a later one is a second hit, and a contact longer than 0.05 s is a carry",
  "Rule 1 (the net): a 2 cm thick wall instead of a mesh of cord; a ball that meets it is stopped by a stiff, strongly damped contact",
  "wind and weather: none; the air is still (sea level, 15 C)"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Return the serve: after the tennis ball has bounced once in your service box, hit it with the racket on your right hand back over the net so that it lands in the singles court on the far side.",
  "rules": "The court is the ITF singles court: 23.77 m long and 8.23 m wide, measured to the outside of the lines, with a service line 6.4 m from the net on each side. The net stands across the middle, 0.914 m high at its centre and rising in a straight line to 1.07 m at the posts, which stand 0.914 m outside the sidelines. The ball is the ITF ball (radius 0.0335 m, 0.0577 kg, with air drag and spin lift); the racket (0.685 m long, 0.30 kg, strung surface 0.33 m by 0.25 m) is fixed to your right hand. 3.0 s after the start the ball is served from behind the far baseline at 30.0 m/s into your right service box. In order: the ball bounces once on your side; your racket hits it (any part of the racket counts); its centre passes the net's plane from your side to the far side, above the net's top edge or outside a post (it may touch the net or a post on the way); and its first contact with the ground after that is inside the far half of the singles court, the lines included (the point under the ball's centre decides; ground contacts less than 0.02 s apart are one bounce): that is the success. You fail if the racket touches the ball before its bounce (a volley), if the ball bounces a second time on your side before your hit, if after your hit the ball touches the ground on your side or outside the far court, if the ball touches any part of you other than the racket at any time after the serve, if the racket touches the ball a second time (contacts less than 0.05 s apart are one hit) or stays on it longer than 0.05 s, if the ball passes the net's plane between the posts below the net's top edge, if any part of you or the racket touches the net or a post, or the ground beyond the net's plane, if anything of you touches the ball before it is served, if you fall, or after 10 s. A fall is: your pelvis lower than 0.35 m above the ground, or any part of you other than your feet touching the ground; the racket may touch the ground.",
  "venue": "You stand as the receiver: 2.75 m to the right of the court's centre line, your pelvis 0.5 m behind your baseline, facing the net (+x), which is 12.385 m ahead of you. Painted on the court, all 0.05 m wide and inside the measurements: the two baselines, the two singles sidelines, the two service lines, the centre service line between them and a 0.10 m centre mark on each baseline; there are no doubles lines. The far baseline is 23.77 m beyond yours. The ball waits where the server strikes it: 2.7 m above the ground, 0.2 m behind the far baseline and 0.8 m to the server's right of the centre mark (your left of the centre line). The serve is flat, with a little topspin; it bounces in your right service box, the one between the centre line and the right sideline, and reaches your baseline about a second after it was struck. The racket's handle lies along your right forearm; its strings face the way your palm faces.",
  "difficulty": "The ball crosses the whole court in about a second and is still moving at more than 15 m/s when it reaches you, so the racket has to be in a path a few centimetres wide at an instant you must predict, and a robot that only walks cannot go far to meet it. A racket merely held in the way sends the ball back at a fraction of its speed, well short of the net twelve metres away: the return needs a real swing through a 0.30 kg racket."
 },
